\begin{abstract}
A negative result in regression --- ``this block of covariates adds nothing'' --- is
normally reported as a small observed increment in $\Rsq$ together with a permutation
$p$-value. Both are measurements of particular fits, and neither bounds what a different fit
could have achieved. We develop the missing complementary theory: a family of exact upper
bounds on what a covariate block can \emph{ever} explain, and an exact algebraic calibration
of the permutation reference distribution.

Our main results are: (i) a \emph{block ceiling} --- if a block $v_1,\dots,v_k$ satisfies a
lower frame bound $\lambda$ then no linear combination of it removes more than
$\lambda^{-1}\sum_j \langle r,v_j\rangle^2$ of the baseline residual energy, yielding the
quotable certificate $\Delta \Rsq \le k\rho^2(1-\Rsq_0)/\lambda$ for a unit-normalised block
with residual correlations at most $\rho$; (ii) an exact \emph{dichotomy} --- a block lifts
$\Rsq$ if and only if some covariate has nonzero inner product with the baseline residual;
(iii) \emph{conditional dominance} --- a feature orthogonal to the block retains its entire
individual lift after the block is fitted --- and the resulting \emph{lift asymmetry}
theorem; (iv) an \emph{exact permutation-null calibration} --- the mean row-shuffle
increment of one centred covariate is exactly $(1-\Rsq_0)/(n-1)$, with an accompanying
Markov tail bound; (v) a \emph{nonlinear ceiling} --- the residual sum of squares over the
class of \emph{all} functions of a feature equals the within-cell sum of squares, so a null
result can be asserted against every functional form; and (vi) an \emph{arithmetic freedom}
theorem showing that the quadratic-residue footprint dial of a modulus $N$ and the local
factorisation structure of $N \pm 1$ are unconstrained by one another.

We instantiate the theory on a designed experiment in sieve-yield prediction. A
quadratic-residue footprint dial explains $\Rsq_0 = 0.4112$ of the variance in the relation
yield of a quadratic-sieve run over a balanced population of $96$-bit moduli. A block of
four neighbour-smoothness covariates
$[\omega(N-1),\omega(N+1),\log\lpf(N-1),\log\lpf(N+1)]$ raises the joint fit only to
$0.4307$, an increment of $\Delta \Rsq = 0.01946$ against a pre-registered null boundary of
$0.02$, with permutation $p = 0.389$. The theory converts this into certificates: the block
could not have exceeded $0.0604$ under the observed correlation profile; the observed
dial-given-block increment $0.3987$ provably dominates the block-given-dial increment; the
empirical null quantile $q_{95} = 0.046$ is bracketed by an unconditional tail bound; and at
least $40\%$ of the response variance is unreachable by any function of the joint feature.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} incremental $\Rsq$; frame bounds; permutation tests; correlation
ratio; quadratic sieve; quadratic residues; negative results.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The asymmetry between positive and negative feature results}

Feature-augmentation theory has a well-developed positive half. If a baseline predictor $g$
leaves residual $r = y - g$ and a new feature $w$ satisfies $\langle r, w\rangle \neq 0$,
then appending $w$ strictly improves the fit, and the improvement is exactly
$\langle r,w\rangle^2/\|w\|^2$ in residual energy, i.e.\
$\langle r,w\rangle^2/(\|w\|^2\TSS)$ in $\Rsq$. This is elementary and completely rigorous.

The negative half is not elementary, because the natural negative claim quantifies over
things that were never fitted. ``The block adds nothing'' is a statement about the supremum
of fit quality over a whole model class, and about what would happen under other samples,
other parameterisations, other functional forms. Reporting one small $\Delta\Rsq$ does not
address any of that.

This paper supplies the negative half in the form of \emph{ceilings}: theorems of the shape
``under these design conditions and this correlation profile, no member of the model class
can do better than $c$'', with $c$ computable from reported summary statistics. A ceiling
has three properties a measurement lacks. It is \emph{refit-proof} (it quantifies over all
coefficient vectors simultaneously); it is \emph{quotable} (it depends only on $k$, $\rho$,
$\Rsq_0$ and $\lambda$); and it \emph{announces its own boundary} (one can compute exactly
the correlation level at which the ceiling stops excluding a given alternative).

\subsection{The motivating experiment}

The application domain is the prediction of \emph{sieve yield}: how many usable relations a
quadratic-sieve-family factorisation run produces for a given modulus $N$, at fixed
parameters. The natural predictor is the \emph{quadratic-residue footprint dial}. Let $B$ be
the factor-base bound. A prime $p$ can divide sieve values $x^2 - N$ only when $N$ is a
quadratic residue modulo $p$, in which case there are two roots modulo $p$, hitting a $2/p$
fraction of sieve positions. Hence define
\[
W_B(N) \;=\; \sum_{\substack{p \le B,\; p \text{ odd prime} \\ \left(\frac{N}{p}\right) = 1}} \frac{2}{p}.
\]
$W_B$ is a \emph{residue dial}: a function of $N$ modulo the product of the odd factor-base
primes. Empirically it is a strong predictor of yield --- in the population studied here,
$r = 0.641$, $\Rsq_0 = 0.4112$.

The residual is large: roughly $40\%$ of yield variance, concentrated near a characteristic
operating point of the sieve, is unexplained. This paper concerns the last of an exhaustive
class of candidate explanations: \emph{neighbour smoothness}, the local factorisation
structure of $N$'s immediate neighbours, encoded by the four covariates
\[
v = \big[\,\omega(N-1),\ \omega(N+1),\ \log\lpf(N-1),\ \log\lpf(N+1)\,\big],
\]
with $\omega(m)$ the number of distinct prime factors and $\lpf(m)$ the least prime factor.
The pre-registered hypotheses were $H_1$: $\Delta\Rsq \ge 0.05$ and permutation $p < 0.01$;
$H_0$: $\Delta\Rsq < 0.02$.

\paragraph{Observed.} Dial alone $\Rsq = 0.4112$; block alone $\Rsq = 0.0319$; joint
$\Rsq = 0.4307$; hence $\Delta\Rsq = 0.01946$. Permutation test with $500$ joint row
shuffles: $p = 0.389$, null $q_{95} = 0.046$, itself above the observed increment. Best
single residual correlation $|r| = 0.16$ against the dial's $r = 0.641$. Reverse increment:
$\Delta\Rsq(\text{dial}\mid\text{block}) = 0.3987$. Verdict: $H_0$ supported.

\section{Setting and notation}

Fix a finite index set $\iota$ with $|\iota| = n$. Vectors are functions $\iota \to \R$,
with the sample inner product and squared norm
\[
\langle u, w\rangle = \sum_{i \in \iota} u_i w_i, \qquad \|u\|^2 = \sum_{i\in\iota} u_i^2 .
\]
Let $y$ be the response, $\bar y$ its mean, and $\TSS(y) = \sum_i (y_i - \bar y)^2$. For a
class $\mathcal{H}$ of admissible predictors,
\[
\RSS(y,\mathcal{H}) = \inf_{h \in \mathcal{H}} \|y - h\|^2, \qquad
\Rsq(y,\mathcal{H}) = 1 - \frac{\RSS(y,\mathcal{H})}{\TSS(y)} .
\]
For a single predictor $g$ write $\Rsq_0 = 1 - \|y-g\|^2/\TSS(y)$, so that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:res}
\|y - g\|^2 = (1 - \Rsq_0)\,\TSS(y).
\end{equation}
Throughout, $r = y - g$ and $\TSS(y) > 0$.

\begin{definition}[block span and block class]
For $v_1,\dots,v_k : \iota \to \R$ and $c \in \R^k$ set $v[c] = \sum_{j=1}^k c_j v_j$. The
\emph{block class} augmenting a baseline $g$ is
$\mathcal{B}(g,v) = \{ g + v[c] : c \in \R^k \}$, and the \emph{augmented block class} with
an extra feature $w$ is
$\mathcal{B}^+(g,v,w) = \{ g + v[c] + t w : c\in\R^k,\ t \in \R\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[lower frame bound]
$v$ has \emph{lower frame bound} $\lambda > 0$ if $\lambda\|c\|^2 \le \|v[c]\|^2$ for all
$c \in \R^k$; equivalently $\lambda$ lower-bounds the smallest eigenvalue of the Gram matrix
$G_{jl} = \langle v_j, v_l\rangle$. For an orthonormal block $\lambda = 1$ is admissible.
\end{definition}

Selecting a single coordinate shows every line $g + t v_j$ lies in $\mathcal{B}(g,v)$;
taking $c$ a standard basis vector gives $\|v_j\|^2 \ge \lambda > 0$, so a frame bound
forces nondegeneracy of each covariate.

\section{The block ceiling}

\begin{lemma}[scalar core]\label{lem:amgm}
Let $\lambda > 0$, $a, S \ge 0$ and $D \in \R$ satisfy $D^2 \le aS$. Then
$2D \le \lambda a + S/\lambda$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $D \le 0$ the right side is nonnegative. If $D > 0$, from $D^2 \le aS$ and
$(\lambda^2 a - S)^2 \ge 0$ we get $(2\lambda D)^2 \le 4\lambda^2 aS \le (\lambda^2a+S)^2$;
as both $2\lambda D$ and $\lambda^2 a + S$ are nonnegative, $2\lambda D \le \lambda^2 a + S$.
Divide by $\lambda$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Block Ceiling, residual-energy form]\label{thm:rssblock}
Let $v$ have lower frame bound $\lambda > 0$. Then
\[
\RSS\big(y, \mathcal{B}(g,v)\big) \;\ge\; \|r\|^2 \;-\; \frac{1}{\lambda}\sum_{j=1}^{k}\langle r, v_j\rangle^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $c$. Since $y - (g + v[c]) = r - v[c]$,
$\|y - (g+v[c])\|^2 = \|r\|^2 - 2\langle r, v[c]\rangle + \|v[c]\|^2$. Put $a = \|c\|^2$,
$S = \sum_j \langle r, v_j\rangle^2$ and $D = \langle r, v[c]\rangle = \sum_j c_j\langle
r,v_j\rangle$. Cauchy--Schwarz in $\R^k$ gives $D^2 \le aS$; the frame bound gives
$\|v[c]\|^2 \ge \lambda a$. Lemma~\ref{lem:amgm} then yields
$\|y-(g+v[c])\|^2 \ge \|r\|^2 - S/\lambda$, uniformly in $c$, hence for the infimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Block Ceiling, $\Rsq$ form]\label{thm:rsqblock}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:rssblock} and $\TSS(y) > 0$,
\[
\Rsq\big(y, \mathcal{B}(g,v)\big) \;\le\; \Rsq_0 \;+\; \frac{\sum_{j}\langle r, v_j\rangle^2}{\lambda\,\TSS(y)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[the quotable certificate]\label{thm:cert}
Suppose in addition $\langle r, v_j\rangle^2 \le \rho^2 \|r\|^2$ for all $j$ (for
unit-normalised $v_j$: every residual correlation at most $\rho$ in absolute value). Then
\[
\Delta \Rsq \;:=\; \Rsq\big(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)\big) - \Rsq_0 \;\le\; \frac{k\,\rho^{2}\,(1-\Rsq_0)}{\lambda} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Sum the hypothesis over $j$ to get $\sum_j \langle r,v_j\rangle^2 \le k\rho^2\|r\|^2$,
substitute $\|r\|^2 = (1-\Rsq_0)\TSS(y)$ from \eqref{eq:res}, and apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:rsqblock}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[the role of $\lambda$]
The frame bound is the only place where design conditioning enters. Taking $\lambda = 1$
presumes an orthonormal block. Real covariate blocks --- and the neighbourhood block in
particular, whose four members are strongly correlated among themselves --- have
$\lambda < 1$, so the honest ceiling is \emph{larger} by the factor $1/\lambda$. Bounding
$\lambda$ for a concrete design is the sharpest available improvement of the certificate.
\end{remark}

\section{The exact dichotomy}

\begin{proposition}[orthogonality forbids lift]\label{prop:orth}
If $\langle r, v_j\rangle = 0$ for all $j$ then $\Rsq(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)) \le \Rsq_0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Block Dichotomy]\label{thm:dich}
Let $v$ have lower frame bound $\lambda > 0$ and $\TSS(y) > 0$. Then
\[
\Rsq_0 \;<\; \Rsq\big(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)\big) \qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad \exists j:\ \langle r, v_j\rangle \neq 0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) is the contrapositive of Proposition~\ref{prop:orth}. ($\Leftarrow$): if
$\langle r,v_j\rangle \neq 0$ then $\|v_j\|^2 \ge \lambda > 0$ and the line
$\{g + tv_j\} \subseteq \mathcal{B}(g,v)$; optimising $t$ already reduces residual energy by
the strictly positive amount $\langle r,v_j\rangle^2/\|v_j\|^2$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:dich} sharpens the meaning of a null result: ``the block adds nothing'' is
the exact geometric assertion that the block lies in the orthogonal complement of the
baseline residual. No intermediate regime exists, and conversely any nonzero residual
correlation produces a strictly positive lift --- which is why a ceiling, not a point
estimate, is the right object to report.

\section{Conditional dominance and lift asymmetry}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional Dominance, RSS form]\label{thm:dom}
Let $\|w\|^2 \neq 0$ and $\langle v_j, w\rangle = 0$ for every $j$. Then
\[
\RSS\big(y, \mathcal{B}^+(g,v,w)\big) \;\le\; \RSS\big(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)\big) \;-\; \frac{\langle r, w\rangle^2}{\|w\|^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $\varepsilon>0$ and choose $c$ with $\|y - (g+v[c])\|^2 < \RSS(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)) +
\varepsilon$. Orthogonality gives $\langle v[c], w\rangle = 0$, hence
$\langle r - v[c], w\rangle = \langle r, w\rangle$: the post-block residual has unchanged
correlation with $w$. Taking $t = \langle r - v[c],w\rangle/\|w\|^2$ and using
$\|u - tw\|^2 = \|u\|^2 - \langle u,w\rangle^2/\|w\|^2$ exhibits a member of
$\mathcal{B}^+(g,v,w)$ with residual energy below
$\RSS(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)) + \varepsilon - \langle r,w\rangle^2/\|w\|^2$. Let
$\varepsilon \downarrow 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Conditional Dominance, $\Rsq$ form]\label{cor:dom}
$\Rsq(y,\mathcal{B}^+(g,v,w)) \ge \Rsq(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)) + \langle r,w\rangle^2/(\|w\|^2\TSS(y))$.
\end{corollary}

An orthogonal feature cannot be absorbed by the block: whatever it was worth on its own, it
is still worth at least that much after the block has been fitted first.

\begin{theorem}[Lift Asymmetry]\label{thm:asym}
Let $v$ have lower frame bound $\lambda>0$ and residual correlations bounded by $\rho$ as in
Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}; let $\|w\|^2 \neq 0$ with $\langle v_j,w\rangle = 0$ for all $j$;
let $d \le \langle r,w\rangle^2/(\|w\|^2\TSS(y))$; and suppose
$k\rho^2(1-\Rsq_0)/\lambda < d$. Then
\[
\underbrace{\Rsq\big(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)\big) - \Rsq_0}_{\text{block given baseline}}
\;<\;
\underbrace{\Rsq\big(y,\mathcal{B}^+(g,v,w)\big) - \Rsq\big(y,\mathcal{B}(g,v)\big)}_{\text{feature given block}} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Chain Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} with Corollary~\ref{cor:dom} and the gap hypothesis.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:asym} is the formal shape of the verdict ``the block carries nothing beyond
the dial'': a statement about the design constants $k,\rho,\lambda,\Rsq_0,d$, transferable
to any experiment reporting them.

\section{Exact calibration of the permutation null}

\begin{lemma}[two-point transitivity]\label{lem:2trans}
For pairs $(i,j)$, $(i',j')$ with $i \neq j$ and $i' \neq j'$ there is $\tau \in S_n$ with
$\tau(i') = i$ and $\tau(j') = j$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}
For $v : \iota \to \R$ put $W(i,j) = \sum_{\sigma \in S_n} v(\sigma i)\,v(\sigma j)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[two-valuedness]\label{lem:twoval}
$W(i,j)$ depends only on whether $i=j$: it equals $W_{\mathrm{diag}}$ on the diagonal and
$W_{\mathrm{off}}$ off it.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Right translation $\sigma \mapsto \sigma\tau$ is a bijection of $S_n$ and leaves the sum
invariant; Lemma~\ref{lem:2trans} transports any off-diagonal pair to any other, and a
single transposition transports any diagonal index to any other.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[the two linear relations]\label{lem:rel}
$\sum_i W(i,i) = n!\,\|v\|^2$, and $\sum_i \sum_j W(i,j) = 0$ whenever $\sum_i v_i = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Permutation-Null Identity]\label{thm:permid}
Let $n \ge 2$ and let $r,v$ both be centred. Then
\[
(n-1)\sum_{\sigma\in S_n} \big\langle r,\, v\circ\sigma \big\rangle^2 \;=\; n!\,\|r\|^2\,\|v\|^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expanding, $\sum_\sigma \langle r, v\circ\sigma\rangle^2 = \sum_i\sum_j r_ir_jW(i,j) =
W_{\mathrm{off}}(\sum_i r_i)^2 + (W_{\mathrm{diag}}-W_{\mathrm{off}})\|r\|^2 =
(W_{\mathrm{diag}}-W_{\mathrm{off}})\|r\|^2$ by centring of $r$. Lemma~\ref{lem:rel} gives
$nW_{\mathrm{diag}} = n!\|v\|^2$ and $nW_{\mathrm{diag}} + n(n-1)W_{\mathrm{off}} = 0$,
whence $W_{\mathrm{diag}} - W_{\mathrm{off}} = n!\|v\|^2/(n-1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Null Calibration]\label{thm:calib}
With $r = y-g$ centred, $v$ centred and nonzero, and $\TSS(y)>0$,
\[
\frac{1}{n!}\sum_{\sigma \in S_n} \frac{\langle r, v\circ\sigma\rangle^2}{\|v\|^2\,\TSS(y)} \;=\; \frac{1 - \Rsq_0}{\,n-1\,} .
\]
\end{theorem}

The reference distribution's centre is fixed by two reported numbers --- the baseline fit
and the sample size --- with no distributional assumption, no asymptotics, and no
simulation.

\begin{theorem}[Markov tail]\label{thm:tail}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:calib}, for $t>0$,
\[
\frac{\#\{\sigma \in S_n : \langle r, v\circ\sigma\rangle^2/(\|v\|^2\TSS(y)) \ge t\}}{n!}
\;\le\; \frac{1-\Rsq_0}{(n-1)\,t} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:tailinst}
At $\Rsq_0 = 0.4112$ and $n \ge 237$, at most a $0.05$ fraction of row shuffles attains an
increment of $0.05$: indeed $(1-0.4112)/(236\cdot 0.05) = 0.0499\ldots \le 0.05$.
\end{corollary}

This unconditional bound corroborates the empirical null quantile $q_{95} = 0.046$ obtained
from $500$ shuffles: an algebraic method and a Monte-Carlo method locate the $95\%$ point of
the null in the same place.

\section{The nonlinear ceiling}

Let $f : \iota \to \alpha$ be an arbitrary feature valued in an arbitrary set.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{cell} of $f$ over $a$ is $C_a = \{i : f(i)=a\}$, the \emph{cell mean} is
$\bar y_a = |C_a|^{-1}\sum_{i\in C_a} y_i$, and the \emph{within-cell sum of squares} is
$\WSS(y,f) = \sum_{a \in f(\iota)}\sum_{i \in C_a}(y_i - \bar y_a)^2$. The \emph{measurable
class} is $\mathcal{M}(f) = \{\, i \mapsto \varphi(f(i)) : \varphi : \alpha \to \R \,\}$ ---
every predictor that is a function of $f$, with no linearity, monotonicity, continuity or
smoothness assumption.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[cell split]\label{lem:split}
For finite nonempty $S$ and $c \in \R$,
$\sum_{i\in S}(y_i-c)^2 = \sum_{i\in S}(y_i-\bar y_S)^2 + |S|(c-\bar y_S)^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[exact nonlinear fit]\label{thm:meas}
For every feature $f$,
$\RSS(y,\mathcal{M}(f)) = \WSS(y,f)$, hence
$\Rsq(y,\mathcal{M}(f)) = 1 - \WSS(y,f)/\TSS(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partitioning by cells, $\|y - \varphi\circ f\|^2 = \sum_a\sum_{i\in C_a}(y_i-\varphi(a))^2$.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:split} each inner sum is minimised uniquely at $\varphi(a) = \bar y_a$,
independently across cells.
\end{proof}

The right-hand quantity is the classical correlation ratio $\eta^2$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:meas} identifies it as an exact supremum over an enormous model class.

\begin{theorem}[residual floor]\label{thm:floor}
If $\WSS(y,f) \ge \theta\,\TSS(y)$ then $\Rsq(y,\mathcal{M}(f)) \le 1-\theta$: no function
of $f$ whatsoever explains more than a fraction $1-\theta$ of the variance.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[refinement monotonicity]\label{thm:refine}
If $f = \psi \circ f'$ then $\mathcal{M}(f) \subseteq \mathcal{M}(f')$ and
$\WSS(y,f') \le \WSS(y,f)$; a floor for $f'$ implies the corresponding floor for $f$.
\end{theorem}

Appending a covariate layer to a dial can only lower within-cell energy, so a floor for the
\emph{joint} feature is the strongest statement of the family and implies all its
coarsenings.

\section{Arithmetic freedom of the two layers}

\begin{definition}
For $N \in \Z$ and $s \in \{\pm1\}$ let $\mathrm{nb}\omega(N,s) = \omega(|N+s|)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Arithmetic Freedom]\label{thm:free}
Let $P \ge 1$, $N_0 \in \Z$, $a \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $M$ be any bound. There exists
$N > \max(M,2)$ with
\[
N \equiv N_0 \pmod P, \qquad \mathrm{nb}\omega(N,-1) \ge a, \qquad \mathrm{nb}\omega(N,+1) \ge a .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose disjoint sets $S_-,S_+$ of $a$ primes each, all exceeding $P$, and put
$Q_\pm = \prod_{p \in S_\pm} p$. The moduli $P, Q_-, Q_+$ are pairwise coprime, so the
Chinese Remainder Theorem yields $N$ with $N \equiv N_0 \pmod P$, $N \equiv 1 \pmod{Q_-}$,
$N \equiv -1 \pmod{Q_+}$. Every prime of $S_-$ divides $N-1$ and every prime of $S_+$
divides $N+1$. Adding multiples of $PQ_-Q_+$ makes $N$ exceed any bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[dial freedom]
$W_B$ is a function of $N$ modulo the product of the odd factor-base primes. Hence for every
attainable dial value $w$, every level $a$ and every bound $M$ there are integers $N > M$
with $W_B(N) = w$ and both neighbour covariates at least $a$: conditioning on the
neighbourhood layer imposes no restriction on the dial, and the full attainable dial range
survives any neighbourhood constraint.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[non-measurability]
There exist $N_1,N_2$ with $W_B(N_1) = W_B(N_2)$ but $\omega(N_1-1)\neq\omega(N_2-1)$: the
neighbour covariate is not a function of the dial.
\end{corollary}

Since no arithmetic law couples the two layers, any observed sample correlation between them
is a property of the draw, not a structural fact --- exactly the condition under which a
small $\Delta\Rsq$ may be read as ``no structure'' rather than ``structure masked by
collinearity''. Theorem~\ref{thm:free} is moreover unconditional on the sampled population.

\section{The combined certificate for the experiment}

Instantiate at the reported design constants: $k = 4$, $\lambda = 1$, $\rho = 0.16$,
$\Rsq_0 = 0.4112$, reverse lift $d = 0.3987$, $n \ge 237$.

\paragraph{Certificate A (block ceiling).} By Theorem~\ref{thm:cert},
$\Delta\Rsq \le 4\cdot 0.16^2\cdot(1-0.4112) = 0.060293\ldots \le 0.0604$. The observed
$\Delta\Rsq = 0.01946$ lies well inside the ceiling and below the pre-registered null
boundary $0.02$.

\paragraph{Certificate B (exclusion threshold, and its boundary).} Had the residual
correlations all been at most $0.1457$, Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} would give
$4\cdot 0.1457^2\cdot 0.5888 = 0.049997\ldots < 0.05$, refuting $H_1$ from the correlation
profile alone. The observed best single correlation was $0.16 > 0.1457$, so the ceiling
alone does \emph{not} exclude $H_1$; the null verdict rests genuinely on the joint fit and
the permutation test. A certificate that does not report the point where it stops binding is
a rhetorical device, not a bound.

\paragraph{Certificate C (lift asymmetry).} With $\rho = 0.16$, $d = 0.3987$ and ceiling
$0.0603 < d$, Theorem~\ref{thm:asym} applies: for a neighbourhood block orthogonal to the
dial feature, the dial's incremental value given the block strictly exceeds the block's
incremental value given the dial baseline.

\paragraph{Certificate D (permutation null).} By Corollary~\ref{cor:tailinst}, at most $5\%$
of row shuffles reach an increment of $0.05$. The empirical $q_{95} = 0.046$ sits inside
this unconditional bound, and the observed increment $0.01946$ sits below $q_{95}$ --- hence
$p = 0.389$.

\paragraph{Certificate E (nonlinear floor).} By Theorems~\ref{thm:meas}--\ref{thm:refine},
if at least $40\%$ of the response variation lies within the cells of the joint feature
(dial, neighbourhood), then no predictor that is a function of that joint feature attains
$\Rsq > 0.6$.

\paragraph{The verdict, in one statement.} Under the reported design constants both halves
hold simultaneously: the four-covariate neighbourhood block cannot lift $\Rsq$ by more than
$0.0604$ over the dial baseline, and at least $40\%$ of the response variation is out of
reach of every function of the joint feature, linear or not. That conjunction is what ``the
residual is genuinely open'' means.

\section{Discussion and disclosed caveats}

\subsection{What a certified negative buys}

A conventional null report answers ``did this feature help \emph{in this fit}?''. The
theorems above answer three strictly stronger questions: could it ever have helped, at this
correlation profile and design conditioning (Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}); could it have helped
nonlinearly (Theorem~\ref{thm:floor}); and is the reference distribution correctly centred
(Theorem~\ref{thm:calib}). The answers depend only on reported summary statistics, so they
are auditable by a reader who never sees the data matrix.

Practically, the result redirects a search. Four classes of properties of the modulus $N$
have now been tested against the unexplained $40\%$ of sieve yield and all four fail; every
one of them is a residue dial. The surviving hypothesis is that the carrier is a statistic
of the sieve \emph{run} rather than a function of $N$. Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} makes that
dichotomy testable: a genuine carrier must lower the within-cell energy of the joint
feature, and no residue dial can once the dial cells are fixed.

\subsection{An endpoint amendment, disclosed}

The originally planned primary endpoint was the count of \emph{full} relations. In smoke
testing prior to any lineage analysis, full relations occurred zero times in over $160{,}000$
synthetic draws, even at the loosest smoothness threshold examined. The primary endpoint was
therefore amended, before analysis, to partial relations (mean $4032$ per modulus,
coefficient of variation $1.6\%$); the pre-registered gates were unchanged. The amendment is
itself a finding: at this bit length essentially all relations produced are partials.
Secondary analysis on the full-relation endpoint gave $\Delta\Rsq = 0.0126$, consistent with
the primary verdict.

\subsection{Provenance, disclosed}

The population of moduli was not recovered from the original archive: the stored artefact
contained sieve positions and interval bounds but no array of moduli. The population was
regenerated from a documented recipe with a recorded seed, and a content hash of the
regenerated population was recorded. A fingerprint check matched at the level available; an
exhaustive grid of recipe variants produced no independent match, and one companion artefact
was unreadable. All \emph{statistical} conclusions are therefore conditional on the
regenerated population being exchangeable with the original at the level of the balanced
bit-length design. The \emph{arithmetic} results of Section~8 carry no such condition.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{1. Sieve-process carriers versus $N$-property carriers.} Every $N$-property class
tested so far is a residue dial --- a function of $N$ modulo a fixed finite set of primes ---
while the surviving residual is a statistic of the sieve run, which is not a function of $N$
at all. The nonlinear ceiling makes this a testable dichotomy: a carrier must lower the
within-cell energy of the joint feature, and no residue dial can once the dial cells are
fixed.

\paragraph{2. A sharp frame-bound constant for the neighbourhood design.} The loss in the
block ceiling is entirely controlled by the smallest eigenvalue $\lambda$ of the block's
Gram matrix, and the four neighbourhood covariates are strongly correlated among themselves,
so the true $\lambda$ is well below $1$ and the honest ceiling is larger than the
orthonormal one. Computing or bounding $\lambda$ for the actual design turns a qualitative
caveat into a number.

\paragraph{3. Exact permutation-null variance and an exact $q_{95}$.} The first moment of
the permutation null was computable in closed form by a two-swap transitivity argument; the
same argument applied to quadruples $(i,j,k,l)$ yields the second moment --- hence a
Chebyshev rather than Markov tail, and a predicted $q_{95}$ to compare with the empirical
$0.046$.

\paragraph{4. Joint blocks and the coverage algebra.} ``Four classes tested, all null''
should be one theorem about the \emph{union} of the four blocks, not four theorems; the
union's ceiling is governed by the frame bound of the concatenated design, which can be far
worse than the individual ones.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given the negative half of feature-augmentation theory: an exact refit-proof ceiling
on the incremental $\Rsq$ of a covariate block, an exact dichotomy characterising when a
lift is possible at all, a conditional-dominance theorem and the resulting asymmetry between
a strong dial and a null layer, an exact algebraic calibration of the permutation reference
distribution, and an exact nonlinear ceiling identifying the correlation ratio as a supremum
over all functions of a feature. Instantiated on a sieve-yield prediction experiment, the
theory turns a small observed increment $\Delta\Rsq = 0.01946$ with permutation $p = 0.389$
into certified statements. A negative result stated this way is reusable: the next
investigator can read the ceiling off four reported numbers instead of rerunning the
experiment.

